[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Understanding the Shared Responsibility Model in Cloud Cyber Security"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"As organizations migrate their infrastructure to cloud environments like AWS, Azure, or Google Cloud Platform, understanding security boundaries is critical. The Shared Responsibility Model defines the security obligations of both the cloud service provider (CSP) and the customer."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"What is the Shared Responsibility Model?"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Shared Responsibility Model divides security duties into two main categories: security OF the cloud and security IN the cloud. While the cloud provider secures the underlying infrastructure, the customer remains responsible for securing their data, applications, and configurations."},{"order":5,"type":"subheading","text":"Responsibilities of the Cloud Provider (Security OF the Cloud)"},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Physical security of data centers, servers, and networking hardware."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Host operating system and virtualization layer patching and updates."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Network infrastructure management, including routers, switches, and edge security."},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"Responsibilities of the Customer (Security IN the Cloud)"},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Identity and Access Management (IAM), including multi-factor authentication and role definitions."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Data classification, encryption at rest, and encryption in transit."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Operating system configuration, network firewalls, and security group rules."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Application security and regular vulnerability patching."},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"Example: Infrastructure as a Service (IaaS) vs. Software as a Service (SaaS)"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"The exact division of labor shifts depending on the cloud service model. In IaaS (e.g., AWS EC2), the customer manages the guest OS and applications. In SaaS (e.g., Microsoft 365), the provider manages nearly everything except user access and data governance."},{"order":16,"type":"subheading","text":"Configuring Secure Cloud Resources via Infrastructure as Code"},{"order":17,"type":"paragraph","text":"To ensure customer-side responsibilities are met consistently, use automated policy enforcement. Below is an example Terraform snippet ensuring an S3 bucket has server-side encryption enabled, fulfilling a critical customer data security obligation."},{"order":18,"type":"code","text":"resource "aws_s3_bucket" "secure_bucket" {
  bucket = "my-company-secure-data"
}

resource "aws_s3_bucket_server_side_encryption_configuration" "encryption" {
  bucket = aws_s3_bucket.secure_bucket.id
  rule {
    apply_server_side_encryption_by_default {
      sse_algorithm = "AES256"
    }
  }
}"},{"order":19,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":20,"type":"paragraph","text":"Failing to understand the Shared Responsibility Model is a leading cause of cloud data breaches. Always verify that your organization is actively managing its designated security domain, particularly around access control, logging, and data encryption."}]
